= Solution
{parent=i}

For a deterministic <partition of an interval> $0=t_0<\cdots<t_m=t$, put $Q_\pi=\sum_j\Delta_jB\,\Delta_j\widetilde B$. <Independence> of the two <Brownian motions> and their <independent increments> gives
$$
\mathbb EQ_\pi=0,\qquad\mathbb EQ_\pi^2=\sum_j(t_j-t_{j-1})^2\leq t|\pi|\longrightarrow0.
$$
The defining sums for <quadratic covariation> therefore converge to zero in the <L2 space> and in <probability>. Hence, with the continuous versions of the <quadratic covariation>,
$$
\boxed{\langle B,\widetilde B\rangle_t=0\quad(t\geq0).}
$$
Equality holds simultaneously for all $t$ by checking rational times and using continuity. For <continuous local martingales>, the angle-bracket notation here agrees with <quadratic covariation>.

= ii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <stochastic process> $W$ starts from zero and has continuous paths. Its increments on disjoint intervals are <independent>, since the corresponding pairs of <Brownian motion> increments are <independent>, and
$$
W_t-W_s\sim N\bigl(0,(\rho^2+1-\rho^2)(t-s)\bigr)=N(0,t-s).
$$
Thus \b[$W$ is a standard <Brownian motion>, including $\rho=\pm1$.] Here the intended <filtration> is the joint natural <filtration>, or another <filtration> relative to which the pair is a two-dimensional <Brownian motion>; <independence> of their path laws alone would not justify arbitrary extra information in a larger <filtration>.

By bilinearity of <quadratic covariation> and part (i),
$$
\boxed{\langle W,B\rangle_t=\rho\langle B\rangle_t+\sqrt{1-\rho^2}\langle\widetilde B,B\rangle_t=\rho t.}
$$

= iii
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <Itô formula> applied to $F(t,x)=e^{t/2}\cos x$ gives exact cancellation of the <drift>:
$$
d(e^{t/2}\cos B_t)=-e^{t/2}\sin B_t\,dB_t.
$$
On every finite interval $[0,T]$, the <Itô integral> is <square-integrable> since $\mathbb E\int_0^Te^s\sin^2B_s\,ds\leq e^T-1$. Therefore \b[$e^{t/2}\cos B_t$ is a true <martingale>, and hence a <local martingale>.]

For the other <stochastic process>, $d(B_t-t^2)=dB_t-2t\,dt$. If $B_t-t^2$ were a <local martingale>, its difference with $B$ would make the continuous <finite-variation process> $-t^2$ a <local martingale>, contradicting the fact that a <continuous finite-variation local martingale is constant>. Also $\mathbb E(B_t-t^2)=-t^2$ is not constant. Thus \b[$B_t-t^2$ is neither a <local martingale> nor a <martingale>.]

= iv
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

On the right-hand side, $\mathbb E_x$ means that the reference <Brownian motion> starts at $B_0=x$; on the left it means the <stochastic differential equation> starts at $X_0=x$. Since $h$ is smooth and compactly supported, $h'$ is bounded and globally <Lipschitz continuous>. Define the <stochastic exponential>
$$
D_t=\exp\!\left(\int_0^t h'(B_s)\,dB_s-\frac12\int_0^t h'(B_s)^2\,ds\right).
$$
The <Novikov condition> holds on every finite horizon $T$, because $\mathbb E\exp(\frac12\int_0^Th'(B_s)^2ds)\leq\exp(T\|h'\|_\infty^2/2)$. Thus $D$ is a true <martingale>. Under $d\mathbb Q=D_Td\mathbb P$, the <Girsanov theorem> makes $\widehat B_t=B_t-x-\int_0^th'(B_s)ds$ a standard <Brownian motion> for $t\leq T$. Consequently $B$ under $\mathbb Q$ solves the prescribed <stochastic differential equation>; the <existence and pathwise uniqueness theorem for a stochastic differential equation> identifies its law with that of $X$.

The <Itô formula> gives $\int_0^t h'(B_s)dB_s=h(B_t)-h(x)-\frac12\int_0^th''(B_s)ds$. Hence, for any bounded measurable $f$,
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E_xf(X_t)=\mathbb E_x\!\left[\exp\!\left(h(B_t)-h(x)-\int_0^tV(B_s)\,ds\right)f(B_t)\right],\quad V=\frac12(h')^2+\frac12h''.}
$$
The finite-horizon argument applies separately to every $t\geq0$.

